{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph"]]},"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/cycleDecomposition()"},"kind":"symbol","primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"cycleDecomposition"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph","text":"FunctionalGraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition","text":"CycleDecomposition","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","anchor":"discussion","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A counting sort of f gives the preimage rows and the in-degrees. Peeling the vertices of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in-degree zero, repeatedly, visits the tree vertices leaves first, summing subtree sizes on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the way, and leaves exactly the cyclic ones; each cycle is then walked from its least","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex. Revisiting the peeled vertices in reverse, parents first, gives every tail length","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and root and a preorder numbering of the trees from the subtree sizes; a counting sort by","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"depth then lays out the level-ancestor table. Nothing recurses or chases a chain of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"dependent reads, so a tail of 10⁷ vertices needs no call stack.","type":"text"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The graph has fewer than 2³¹ vertices."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside","style":"note","name":"Precondition"}]}],"abstract":[{"text":"The cycles of the graph, and every vertex’s tail, root and preimages. O(n) time, with no","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"allocation per vertex. The result holds 5n + 3 ","type":"text"},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"s (two ","type":"text"},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" arrays for the preimage","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"rows, one for the vertices by depth, a 12-byte record per vertex and a 4-byte preorder","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"number beside each vertex by depth), plus one per cycle and one per unit of the longest","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"tail; building it takes 3n more for a moment (two 12-byte entries per vertex).","type":"text"}],"metadata":{"role":"symbol","roleHeading":"Instance Method","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"cycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FunctionalGraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"CycleDecomposition","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV","kind":"typeIdentifier"}],"extendedModule":"FunctionalGraphs","title":"cycleDecomposition()","symbolKind":"method","externalID":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18cycleDecompositionAC05CycleE0VyF","modules":[{"name":"FunctionalGraphs"}]},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition()"]}],"references":{"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs":{"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","role":"collection","kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs","title":"FunctionalGraphs","abstract":[]},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph/cycleDecomposition()":{"url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition()","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/cycleDecomposition()","title":"cycleDecomposition()","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"cycleDecomposition"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV","text":"FunctionalGraph"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV","text":"CycleDecomposition"}],"role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The cycles of the graph, and every vertex’s tail, root and preimages. O(n) time, with no","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"allocation per vertex. The result holds 5n + 3 ","type":"text"},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"s (two ","type":"text"},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" arrays for the preimage","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"rows, one for the vertices by depth, a 12-byte record per vertex and a 4-byte preorder","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"number beside each vertex by depth), plus one per cycle and one per unit of the longest","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"tail; building it takes 3n more for a moment (two 12-byte entries per vertex).","type":"text"}]},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph/CycleDecomposition":{"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every cycle of a functional graph, and for each vertex its tail and the cycle it reaches, in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Flajolet and Odlyzko’s terms: walking from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":", the first vertex on a cycle is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"root(of: v)"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tailLength(of: v)"},{"type":"text","text":" steps away, on a cycle of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cycleLength(of: v)"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertices. For a permutation, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cycles"},{"type":"text","text":" is its cycle decomposition and every tail is zero."}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"CycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"FunctionalGraph.CycleDecomposition","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"FunctionalGraph"}],"url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"The graph of a function f on the vertices ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<n"},{"text":": every vertex ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"text":" has exactly one out-edge,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v → f(v)"},{"text":", and a fixed point is a self-loop. Also called a successor graph.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"FunctionalGraph"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph","kind":"symbol","title":"FunctionalGraph","role":"symbol"}}}